WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.112 --> 00:00:06.320
DREW BRADSTOCK: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.300
Thanks for joining us
today for a session.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.980
We're going to talk about
what's new as GKE turns 5.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.890
I'm Drew Bradstock,
product leader for GKE.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.440
And joining me today
is Alex Zakonov,

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.900
engineering director for GKE.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.110
Now it's pretty amazing to
think that we're celebrating

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.490
GKE's fifth birthday today.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.850
Now, I know we may
have promised cake,

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.830
but unfortunately,
that cake is a lie.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:31.330
And what you're
going to put up with

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.430
is just this virtual version.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:35.180
Alex and I had talked
about whether or not

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.900
it was a good idea to
sing happy birthday to GKE

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.280
in this session.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.860
But thankfully, due to the
fact we're both awful singers,

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.030
we declined.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:46.480
So with that, I'd like
to turn it over to Alex.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.530
ALEX ZAKONOV: Welcome.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.570
Today's an amazing day for us.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.840
And we celebrated together
with all our customers,

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.770
customers who use GKE to
scale their applications

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:01.000
to meet huge demands of
[INAUDIBLE] Black Friday, Cyber

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.710
Monday, customers who use
GKE to run their banking

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.730
and financial
applications, customers

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:12.520
who use GKE to do
medical research

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.130
or learn how to optimize
self-driving cars.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.860
That's an amazing day for us.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:26.040
The journey towards that
day started way in the past.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.350
It goes back to
2006, when Google

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:35.190
open-sourced the modern
concept of Linux containers.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.630
That's how this started.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:42.400
In 2012, Google was ready
to take the next step

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.030
and to start the work
on bringing in container

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:50.200
management at scale
and all experience

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.860
that Google internally
had with the technology

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.050
to the outside world.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:02:00.500
And in 2015, Kubernetes
was unveiled,

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.940
setting the new
standard on what it

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.560
means to run cloud-native
infrastructure and cloud-native

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.750
applications.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:14.040
A year later, in
August 2015, GKE

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.360
was born, making it
simple and available

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:23.280
for customers to run
Kubernetes clusters.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.650
Customers loved the experience
with GKE and Kubernetes.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.720
But they told us
that it's not enough.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.430
They told that they gap from
legacy on-prem applications

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:41.290
to cloud-native applications
running in the modern cloud

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:46.090
is too big, that they need
help on their journey.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.630
And we listened to them.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:52.460
And in 2018, we
announced GKE on prem,

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:57.480
bringing in the power of
GKE to customer data centers

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.660
and allowing them to
modernize in place.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.300
Customers told us repeatedly
that GKE is the gold standard.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.260
And they need to
make sure that they

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.860
can leverage that gold standard
no matter where they are.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:22.260
And this year, we announced GKE
on AWS, bringing in GKE power

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.580
to multi-cloud
customers who want

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:29.440
to have consistent, high-quality
Kubernetes experience

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.415
across on-prem and multi-cloud.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.460
With that, we have
an amazing platform,

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.250
but it's only
started the journey.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.170
There is way more to do.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:49.190
And if we look on
the next five years,

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:53.700
we see a world where every
workload built around

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.820
on top of containers
and on top of Kubernetes

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.260
no matter if it's
[INAUDIBLE] or real-time,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.080
no matter if it's
a legacy workflow

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.030
or modern cloud-native
application.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.950
And that's a high
demand that we need

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:15.510
to meet, making
sure that we have

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.199
reliable, secure,
scalable platform

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:22.920
to run any type of workloads.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.540
So how are we going
to meet that demand?

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.790
So as Google continues
heavily invest in open source

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:38.610
Kubernetes, helping with scale,
security, and reliability

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:44.410
of Kubernetes, Google is
the number-one contributor

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:48.970
of investing all other
major cloud providers

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.730
as well as other
companies that support

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:53.680
Kubernetes distributions.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.400
Google funds [INAUDIBLE]
for Kubernetes development.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.660
Kubernetes is built and
distributed on GKE itself.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:06.230
And all Kubernetes
testing is run on GKE.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:14.030
This all said, Kubernetes is
still [? current. ?] Kubernetes

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:17.230
is extremely powerful platform.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:20.890
That power comes
with responsibility.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:27.080
And no offense to Kelsey,
but Kubernetes shouldn't

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.485
have to be the hard way.

00:05:29.485 --> 00:05:33.950
And a huge thank you to
Kelsey and our DevRel team

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:37.680
for bringing all the
feedback on what we

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.360
need to simplify in Kubernetes.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:46.920
And GKE is our answer to
simplifying Kubernetes.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:52.060
With GKE, we take
complexity of Kubernetes,

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.930
and we are shifting
it from being

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:59.684
customer's responsibility to
being Google's responsibility.

00:05:59.684 --> 00:06:03.950
With GKE, Google SRE team
is responsible for creating

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:08.400
GKE clusters in highly
reliable and scalable way.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.050
Google's security team is
responsible for analyzing

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.250
and addressing all security
threats across the full stack,

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:21.560
starting from GKE Kubernetes
itself, going to Linux,

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.960
and going even to our
language itself, which is Go.

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:31.760
[INAUDIBLE] and Google
development team

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.860
is responsible for taking
all the knowledge that we

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:40.810
get from running millions of
workloads on top of containers

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:44.650
and converting it in a
set of new functionality

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.840
and innovation
for all customers.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.450
One concrete example
of that innovation

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.470
is what we do is this
auto-scaling functionality.

00:06:56.170 --> 00:07:01.690
Google constantly watches
internal and external workloads

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:08.510
running on top of
containers and builds

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.450
machine learning
predictable models

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:16.070
on how to scale workloads
in the most efficient way.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.640
And we bring that
knowledge, that intelligence

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.640
to our customers.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:26.040
GKE allows the automatically
scaled infrastructure,

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:29.630
supporting the result
of node auto-scaling,

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:33.330
as well as dynamically
scale your workloads,

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:36.290
supporting both the result
of port auto-scaling

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:38.930
as well as workload
auto-scaling,

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:42.830
making sure that your workloads
run efficiently and can

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:45.890
meet high-scale demands.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:49.910
A concrete example of
that is our partnership

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:55.420
with Tokopedia, a major
Indonesian retailer that

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:59.370
needs to meet
high-scale requirements

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:01.850
of their peak retail season.

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:06.340
Tokopedia partnered with Google
to optimize their environment

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.140
to make sure that,
on one side, they can

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.390
run in a cost-effective way.

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:14.540
But on the other side,
when their customer traffic

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:17.870
increases, they can
dynamically meet those demands.

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:24.840
Another major
dimension is scale.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:30.610
As new major workloads move
to containers in Kubernetes,

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:34.270
the demand for scale increases.

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:40.000
This year, GKE announced
15,000-node support

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:45.370
for Kubernetes clusters
riding on top of GKE.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:47.170
And that was done
in a partnership

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:51.930
with our top customers,
such as Bayer Crop Science,

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:58.000
that uses GKE to run
genotype analysis,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:01.900
analyzing 15 billion
genotypes per hour,

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:06.010
as well as Twitter,
who's using GKE to power

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:10.960
a real-time communication
platform across the world.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.070
Please attend Twitter's
session to learn more about how

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:16.160
GKE is using--

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:20.600
how Twitter is using GKE to move
their [INAUDIBLE] and Aurora

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:24.910
workloads and megaclusters to
Kubernetes and GKE clusters.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:35.860
Another interesting use
case is gaming, where GKE

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:40.560
is partnered with Phoenix Labs.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.370
With gaming
applications, whenever

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:46.330
you launch a new title,
it's extremely hard, almost

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:51.340
impossible, to predict exact
scale that you will need.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:54.040
As a result, in
many cases, you need

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:58.260
to design [INAUDIBLE]
for optimal scalability.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:02.740
Phoenix Labs partnered
with Google and GKE team

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:05.860
around the launch
of their new title.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.940
We designed the
infrastructure to make sure

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:12.390
that it will meet
their scaling needs

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:17.360
as well as partnered with them
on reviewing best practices

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:20.000
for their application layer.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:26.300
As a result of that, whenever
[INAUDIBLE] title was large.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:29.850
On the first week,
number of gamers

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.330
crossed 4 million gamers.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:37.410
We are able to meet those
demands without [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:40.180
Next, we are taking
all that co-innovation

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.610
with our top customers
and bringing it

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:44.620
to the next wave
of our customers.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:47.342
And Drew will talk
more about that.

00:10:47.342 --> 00:10:48.550
DREW BRADSTOCK: Thanks, Alex.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:50.465
It's great to see that
level of partnership

00:10:50.465 --> 00:10:51.340
with their customers.

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:53.870
Because that has been
the heart of GKE.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:56.830
If we go way back to
the very beginning,

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.260
the first phase of
GKE and Kubernetes

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.370
was really around the builders.

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:03.970
These are companies put a
heavy [INAUDIBLE] investment

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:06.640
and their own time
into building new core

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.680
functionality into Kubernetes.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.900
And we were able to
leverage that through GKE.

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:13.070
From there, we moved
to the next phase,

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:14.680
which is the cloud scalers.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:18.580
That's where great firms
such as Shopify and Spotify

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.370
work with Google use
GKE to be able to obtain

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:23.380
that huge, massive,
global scale they

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:27.250
need to grow their businesses
to where they are in 2020.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:31.160
From there, we've entered a very
different phase of Kubernetes.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:32.890
This is where the
previous companies

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:35.020
were very cloud-native,
started in the cloud,

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:36.430
and grew in the cloud.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:40.090
Instead, we're working with a
far larger number of customers.

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:43.540
We're actually moving from on
prem, are moving from VMware,

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.070
into containers.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.650
And the needs of these
customers, what many of you

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:51.100
need, is very different from
the customers at the beginning.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:54.310
Instead, they're looking
for a lot more simplicity.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:56.350
Rather than having
50 different options

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.450
to do something and
full choice, they

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:00.100
want to have it a
lot more automated.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:01.350
They want it more opinionated.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.290
And they want more
help from Google

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:05.770
so they're not having
to build it themselves.

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:08.350
And this is where we get to
the next phase of Kubernetes

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:12.040
adoption and where
we're really taking GKE.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:14.770
This is the wide-scale
adoption of Kubernetes

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:17.860
across enterprises, just
like Alex mentioned.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.230
And what they're really
looking for is the ability

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:24.310
to take all the learnings Google
has across its massive fleet

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:28.960
of both internal systems as well
as GKE as a whole, globally,

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:30.940
and use that to be
able to automate much

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:33.220
of what they do to
run the system so they

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.190
don't have to worry about it.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:37.660
Now a great example
of this change

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:40.330
is actually what we've seen
with the support of Windows.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:44.430
Previously, and for a long
time, GKE was just Linux.

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:45.180
And this is great.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:46.720
It allowed great scale.

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:50.440
But the challenge was much
of a business's workloads,

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.720
especially those that didn't
start in the cloud, were

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:55.390
trapped on Windows
servers and VMware,

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:58.450
which means that modernization
journey a lot easier.

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:01.030
By supporting Windows
as a first-class citizen

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:04.420
and having it fully in
GKE, it's really opened up

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:06.820
a lot of the migration
into the cloud [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:10.450
and also consolidation
onto Kubernetes clusters.

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:12.550
Hand in hand with
that though, it's

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:14.410
not just Kubernetes support.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:17.620
It's help to be able to
migrate those VM-based apps

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:20.440
into containers, which
can be quite a struggle.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:22.240
This is where
Migrate for Anthos,

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:25.040
which also works great
for Migrate with GKE such

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.660
that you don't need Anthos,
to help you, in an automated

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:32.560
fashion, move your applications
from VM containers,

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:37.030
be it in the cloud or
on prem, into GKE--

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.490
this takes a lot of the
risk, a lot of the time,

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:40.750
and a lot of the difficulty.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:44.530
And instead, the tooling does
it for you, which can greatly

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:48.540
ramp up your journey into GKE.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:52.090
Now from there, we need to
make the entire thing simple.

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:55.400
One of the things I get
often from customers is, hey,

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:57.100
we're on Kubernetes.

00:13:57.100 --> 00:13:59.530
And it's almost an
implied question mark.

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:01.150
So what do you go from there?

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:02.800
Because that first
step of creating

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:05.410
a cluster can be
quite difficult.

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:07.870
And that's been a huge
amount of our investment

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:11.610
over the past year and a half
is to simplify that experience.

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:14.890
Take creating the cluster for
the first time in auto way--

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:18.010
automate away much of what
you'd normally be doing.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:21.550
So rather than have it being 20
minutes and many, many steps,

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:23.800
instead, you can be live
in your first Kubernetes

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:25.710
cluster in only minutes.

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:29.080
And this really shifts
Kubernetes from the hard away.

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:32.228
And I personally learned from
Kelsey Hightower's great book

00:14:32.228 --> 00:14:33.770
and went through
that lab experience.

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:35.160
So thank you, Kelsey.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:38.320
But honestly, I don't want
many large enterprises

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:39.890
wanting to have to do that.

00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:42.490
They instead want to be
able to do this smoothly

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:44.830
so they can go from
their first dev cluster,

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.080
to their first
production cluster,

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:49.270
to their second production
cluster, to their third,

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:52.840
and so on very smoothly, without
all this additional effort

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:54.070
for each one.

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:56.210
And that's where GKE
really stands out.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:59.500
And you'll continue to see
more and more simplification

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:02.110
and GKE doing more
and more of Kubernetes

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:04.500
so you have to do less.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.480
Now just like your initial
experience shouldn't be hard,

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:10.555
day 2 operations shouldn't
be scary, but unfortunately

00:15:10.555 --> 00:15:12.070
are for many people.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:14.380
This means, how do you
keep up with patching?

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:16.930
How do you handle upgrades,
be it for stateless

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:18.520
or stateful workloads?

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:21.010
How do you handle
security or issues

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:22.780
that pop up at the last minute?

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:25.930
This can often be a very
time-consuming initiative

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:28.850
that can slow down those
full production rollouts.

00:15:28.850 --> 00:15:30.430
That's why,
specifically, we have

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:32.940
made a very strong
investment on this

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.320
in leveraging what we've
done with our SRE teams

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:38.560
and [INAUDIBLE] to look
across the entire fleet

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:40.630
to simplify things for you.

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:42.910
Great example of this
is release channels.

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:45.010
What this allows
us to do is pick

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.140
a GA version of Kubernetes--

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:49.740
so rapid, regular, and
stable are all GA--

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:52.600
and decide, based on
your risk profile,

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:55.240
or your particular
workload's risk profile,

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:57.010
which versions you'd want.

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.230
Now what I mean by
this is not everyone

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.360
wants to be
bleeding-edge, and not

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.810
everyone wants to be back
a couple of releases.

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:06.015
So if you've got a
workload that can never

00:16:06.015 --> 00:16:07.390
afford to be going
down and you'd

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:10.750
like a much longer soak time
with this version of Kubernetes

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:13.240
out in the wild
for a long period,

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:16.130
stable is the best bet for you.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:19.070
But if you want something
a little more current,

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:20.210
you can use regular.

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:22.890
If you want the latest and
greatest of Kubernetes--

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:25.600
and we certify the newest
version out and all its tasks

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:26.860
very quickly--

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.200
you can choose rapid.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:32.230
Now the cool thing about
this is as GKE certifies

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:34.780
the next version in
rapid, regular, or stable,

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:38.290
we move it up for you and
automatically do the upgrades.

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:39.130
And this is huge.

00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:41.800
Because it takes that risk,
takes all the security

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:44.840
patching, takes all the
testing out of your hands

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:48.040
and allows us to do it, and also
look across the entire fleet

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:50.020
so you benefit
from that as well.

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:53.200
And that makes day 2 a lot
simpler and a lot more stable

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:55.300
no matter who you are.

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.610
Really good example of
this is actually the work

00:16:57.610 --> 00:16:59.090
that FACEIT did with us.

00:16:59.090 --> 00:17:01.540
And FACEIT runs a
massive platform

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:03.670
for online multiplayer games.

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:06.130
And before they took
advantage of surge upgrades,

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:07.660
they were spending
about two weeks

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.018
upgrading their environments.

00:17:09.018 --> 00:17:10.810
For any of you who have
lived through this,

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.890
you know how scary upgrades
can be, be it databases

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:15.250
or be it Kubernetes.

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:17.740
And being able to reduce
the risk and leverage

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:20.140
automated and
tested tools allowed

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:23.440
FACEIT to go from that two
weeks to less than a day.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:26.550
The important thing
for this is less risk.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:29.620
And also, it freed up their
team to work on other tasks

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:31.960
rather than focusing on
upgrades and building

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:34.658
all their own scripts, which,
over time, can be brittle.

00:17:34.658 --> 00:17:36.700
And that's not where you
want your best engineers

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:39.880
in your platform
team to focus on.

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:41.470
Now if you look at
where we're going,

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:43.790
we're really just
getting started.

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:46.780
As we've spoken about,
a heavy focus for GKE

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:49.000
has been taking the
complexity of Kubernetes

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:50.920
and greatly simplifying it.

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:53.170
But that's not the
only thing we're doing.

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:55.330
If you look at scaling,
traditionally, people

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:57.700
talk about how far they can go.

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:00.753
And what's been key for
that, as Alex spoke about,

00:18:00.753 --> 00:18:02.920
is actually the fact that
it was done in partnership

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:05.360
with customers like
Bayer and Twitter.

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:06.850
So we were running
real workloads,

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:08.920
and seeing what was
happening with them,

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:12.310
and testing all parts of
GKE so that we can scale

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:15.730
at small workloads, large
workloads, or any combination

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:17.630
of what you can throw at us.

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:20.680
Now the other side of this
coin is, how do we actually

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:22.240
optimize cost?

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:24.640
It's great if you can
scale up and handle

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:27.310
that maximum
capacity at any time.

00:18:27.310 --> 00:18:29.920
But truly, you want to
be able to scale down

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.110
to meet the needs of
your business as well,

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:34.480
to free up compute and
network costs to be

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:36.430
able to really save you money.

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:38.320
And this is where the
team that's doing this

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:42.710
has focus on that scale-down
and optimizing for costs.

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:45.730
This can be with efficient VMs,
a new offering for GCP that

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:47.980
brings down your overall
compute cost, where

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:51.190
GKE can do that certification
and testing ahead so that you

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:54.640
don't have to, or working
in partnership with us

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:57.760
to take a workload that
completely changes globally

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:01.210
depending on time of day, which
we actually did with OpenX.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.350
And just like the
session with Twitter,

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:05.500
there's a really
great one with OpenX

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:09.520
on what they did to handle a
global advertising application

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:11.950
and control costs
at the same time.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:14.200
Now along with that,
there continues

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:16.330
to be a lot of
investment in security.

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:17.420
This is key.

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:19.750
This is something you want
some of the global fleet

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:22.720
to be taking responsibility
for and constantly

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:25.390
looking at harding your
application for you

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:27.080
rather than worrying about it.

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:29.470
So I think, as you
look at Kubernetes,

00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:32.320
really, our innovation
is just getting started.

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:36.820
And I'd love to turn it
over to Alex Zakonov now.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:38.370
ALEX ZAKONOV: Thank you, Drew.

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:42.120
Innovation is the
core of Google DNA.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:46.020
And we had amazing five
years of innovation.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:47.880
And we are looking
for the next five

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:51.520
years of bringing more
innovation to you,

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:54.090
the customer.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:58.650
Please stay tuned for
additional announcements from us

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:02.950
in Q4 this year and
next year, letting

00:20:02.950 --> 00:20:09.700
you simplify your operation,
to scale your workloads to run

00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:12.790
efficiently, as
well as making sure

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:15.700
that all your enterprise
security needs on compliance

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:17.450
is met.

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:19.140
Thank you.

